# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## What this repository is

`{{ model_name }}` — a MEDS model generated from
[MEDS_model_template](https://github.com/mmcdermott/MEDS_model_template) with the **`{{ profile }}`**
profile. The template generated the *command DAG*, the artifact discipline and the conformance suite.
**It generated no model:** `src/{{ model_slug }}/model.py` is a stub whose hooks raise.

This DAG's chain:

{% if profile == 'supervised' %}`preprocess_data` → `supervised_train` → `predict`
{% elif profile == 'finetune' %}`preprocess_data` → `pretrain` → `supervised_train` → `predict`
{% elif profile == 'probe' %}`preprocess_data` → `pretrain` → `infer` (embeddings) → `supervised_train` (a head on the frozen features) → `predict`
{% elif profile == 'zero_shot_direct' %}`preprocess_data` → `pretrain` → `predict` (straight from the pretrained model)
{% elif profile == 'zero_shot_materialized' %}`preprocess_data` → `pretrain` → `infer` (task scores) → `predict` (from those artifacts)
{% elif profile == 'packaged' %}`preprocess_data` → `predict` (weights ship with the repository; nothing trains)
{% else %}whatever `meds-model commands` lists — run it, and read `src/{{ model_slug }}/configs/profile/default.yaml`
{% endif %}
`README.md` documents the commands, their arguments and the artifact layout — read it for usage. This
file covers what is easy to get wrong from the inside.

## Porting an existing model? Read the procedure first

If the task is to reimplement a published model (a package, a paper repo) here, read
[`docs/PORTING-A-MODEL.md`](docs/PORTING-A-MODEL.md) **in full before writing any code**. It is not
background: it defines a required deliverable — `IMPLEMENTATION_REPORT.md`, a ledger recording every
element of the source as ported, adapted or omitted — and an inventory step that comes before the first
line of code.

It exists because the first port done this way reproduced the architecture and the training objective and
silently dropped four other things: the vocabulary adaptation that makes the model work at all, the
optimiser's weight-decay grouping, the LR schedule, and the LR value. Each omission was afterwards
described as a deliberate design difference. **Dropping a source element because it is awkward for the
test fixture is the failure mode that document exists to prevent** — fix the fixture instead.

## Ownership: what you write, and what `copier update` overwrites

| Path | Owner | On `copier update` |
|---|---|---|
| `src/{{ model_slug }}/model.py` | you | never touched (`_skip_if_exists`) |
{% if profile in ['zero_shot_direct', 'packaged'] %}| `src/{{ model_slug }}/predict.py` | you | never touched |
{% endif %}| `src/{{ model_slug }}/commands.py` | you | never touched |
| `src/{{ model_slug }}/configs/{model,paths,profile}/` | you | never touched |
| `src/{{ model_slug }}/configs/` (other groups), `__main__.py` | template | 3-way merged |
| `src/meds_model_base/` | template | **overwritten** |

`src/meds_model_base/` is the vendored contract: the command ABCs and source arbitration, the
`meds-model` dispatcher, the manifest layer, the default command implementations, the schemas, and a
small MEDS-batch adapter layer. It contains no models. **Do not edit it to make a model fit** — the next
`copier update` discards the change. If the contract genuinely blocks the model, that is a finding to
raise upstream and to record, not a local patch.

It also carries two pieces of template-managed *tooling* that are deliberately not part of the command
contract: `meds_model_base.testing` (the conformance harness) and `meds_model_base.meds_dev`
(`meds-model-add-to-meds-dev`). Neither may become a sixth command.

## The stub is deliberate

`model.py` declares the hooks this DAG calls — its module docstring lists exactly which — and each raises
`NotImplementedError`. The class attribute `is_stub = True` is the marker.

Delete `is_stub` once the model is real. The conformance tests that need a model call
`meds_model_base.testing.skip_if_stub` and skip until it is gone; they are the specification the model
has to satisfy, not scaffolding to delete. A fresh repository is therefore green *and* honest — pass
`-rs` to see what is skipping and why:

```bash
uv run pytest -m "not slow" -rs   # CLI, workspace, arbitration, and the end-to-end conformance test
uv run pytest -m slow -rs         # designed-signal learnability, with a negative control
uv run pytest -m meds_dev -rs     # opt-in: clones MEDS-DEV and runs this model through it
```

**"All tests pass" while the stub is in place means the model was never tested.** Report skip counts
alongside pass counts, and say which tier was skipping.

## Rules that look like details and are not

Each of these otherwise produces output that looks correct:

- **The dispatcher calls `command(cfg)`, never `command.run(cfg)`.** `__call__` runs `validate()`, which
  arbitrates the sources and caches the result on `self.source`. Calling `run` directly bypasses
  arbitration entirely.
- **Sources are alternatives, not layers.** Supplying two is an error; supplying one an implementation
  does not list in `supported_sources` is an error. There is no precedence order, and adding one is not a
  fix.
- **Manifests are read, not just written.** `_runtime.load_trained_module` takes `module_class` from the
  artifact's manifest rather than `cfg.model._target_`, because a checkpoint is loadable only by the
  class that wrote it; `resolve_workspace` recovers `input_data_dir` from the source model's manifest.
  That is why `predict` needs no workspace argument — adding one invites the mismatch the manifest
  exists to prevent.
- **`predict` never reads ground truth.** `_runtime.load_index` drops `boolean_value`, so
  `batch.boolean_value` is absent at prediction time. Labels are joined back onto the output afterwards,
  because `meds-evaluation` scores a predictions file in isolation.
- **`predict` enforces coverage.** Every index timepoint in the selected splits is scored or the command
  fails; `n_expected` / `n_written` are recorded per split. Narrow the request with `splits=[held_out]`
  rather than writing a short file.
- **A warm start that matches zero parameters raises.** Non-strict checkpoint loading is what makes a
  fresh head possible; it also silently yields a randomly initialised model when an encoder is renamed.
- **Publish artifacts through `write_artifact()`.** It stages into a temp sibling and renames into place,
  writing `manifest.yaml` last — so a directory that exists is a directory that finished.
- **Import weight is load-bearing.** `commands/base.py`, `manifest.py` and `dispatch.py` are torch-free
  so `meds-model commands` and `--help` stay cheap, and `commands/__init__.py` imports the heavy
  implementations lazily through a module-level `__getattr__`. A CLI smoke test depends on it; don't
  hoist those imports.
- **`schemas.py` omits `from __future__ import annotations` on purpose.** `flexible_schema` reads live
  `Required(...)` descriptors off class annotations, and PEP 563 stringization breaks schema
  construction.

## Configuration

Every command is a Hydra application over `src/{{ model_slug }}/configs/`, with one root config per
command, named after it. Any field is overridable on the command line
(`meds-model supervised_train trainer.max_epochs=50 model.d_model=256`); shared locations live in
`paths/default.yaml`.

Configure through the config tree, not in Python: a logger constructed inside `model.py` is invisible to
`logger=`, and a hyperparameter hardcoded there is invisible to a sweep and absent from the run's
recorded config.

## Before reporting the work as done

- `uv run pytest -m "not slow" -rs` and `uv run pytest -m slow -rs`, with pass *and* skip counts reported.
- `uv run ruff check .` and `uv run ruff format --check .` (pre-commit runs both).
- If anything was ported from another package, `IMPLEMENTATION_REPORT.md` exists and satisfies the
  Step 7 checklist in `docs/PORTING-A-MODEL.md`.
